            <listPerson>
                <person xml:id="BOO03" sex="2">
                    <persName>
                        <roleName/>
                        <forename>Isabel</forename>
                        <surname>Booth</surname>
                        <addName>née Baxter</addName>
                    </persName>
                    <birth when="1000">
                        <placeName> </placeName>
                    </birth>
                    <death when="1000"/>
                    <occupation/>
                    <note type="editorial">
                        <p>Isabel Booth was the wife of writer <persName key="BOO01">David Booth</persName> and the daughter of 
    <persName key="BAX01">William Baxter</persName>. <persName key="SHE02">Mary Shelley</persName> went to live with
    <persName key="BAX01">Baxter's</persName> family and became good friends with Isabel.</p>
                        <p>Booth had been married to Isabel's sister and had married Isabel 
      after her death. Locke (291) points to this being 'in defiance of both his church and the law.'
      Booth prevented any contact betwen Isabel and Mary and Shelley, even after the Shelley's marriage. 
      Locke describes him as 'by all accounts a husband bad-tempered to the point of cruelty.'</p>
                    </note>
                    <bibl type="MS"> MS. Abinger c. 46, fols. 123-4: 1 November 1823</bibl>
                    <bibl type="MS"> MS. Abinger c. 48, fol. 9-10: 24 June 1828 </bibl>
                </person>
            </listPerson>
